WEBVTT
TITLE File Organisation And Indexing — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A database file lives on disk in blocks. One block is one disk read, and a disk read is thousands of times slower than a memory read.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Without an index, finding one record means reading blocks until it appears. On average that is half the file: five thousand block reads here.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
An index is a second, much smaller file: sorted (key, block pointer) entries, organised as a tree whose nodes are blocks too.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Three index blocks plus the one record block: four reads instead of five thousand. That ratio is the whole reason this topic exists.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
How records are laid out is a separate decision from how they are indexed. Heap: append anywhere, search by scanning.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
A sorted file allows a sparse index: one entry per block, pointing at that block's first key. A heap file needs a dense index: one entry per record.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Clustered: the file is sorted on the index key, so matching records sit in neighbouring blocks. Unclustered: each match can be on any block.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The index is a file too, so it can be indexed. That is a multilevel index, and its top level is usually small enough to stay in memory.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
The order p is the maximum number of children a node may have. Such a node holds p-1 keys and p pointers, and each key carries a record pointer.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
In a B-tree each key appears exactly once in the whole tree, so a search can stop at an internal node — the record is right there.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Every leaf is at the same depth. That is what "balanced" means, and it is the answer GATE 2016 Set 2 keys: equal root-to-leaf path lengths.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Occupancy: every node except the root holds between ceil(p/2)-1 and p-1 keys. The root is exempt, and may hold as little as one key.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The B+ tree moves every record pointer down to the leaves. Internal nodes keep only separators, so they hold more keys per block.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
A separator equals the smallest key in the subtree to its right. This is the copy-up rule GATE 2025 Set 2 states outright.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The leaves are chained left to right, in key order. A range scan is one descent plus a walk along that chain.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So every successful search costs exactly the height, leaves included, and a key may appear twice: once in a leaf, once as a separator above it.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Order is not chosen, it is solved. A node must fit in one block: p child pointers plus p-1 keys, each of known size.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
GATE 2015 Set 3: block 1024, key 12, pointer 8. So 8p + 12(p-1) is at most 1024, giving p at most 51, so at most 50 keys.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
A leaf holds (key, record pointer) pairs plus one pointer to the next leaf. GATE 2007: 16n + 6 is at most 1024, so n is 63.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
GATE 2020: order 205, leaf capacity 204, one million records, so three levels, so three index reads plus one for the record: four.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Insertion begins with one descent to the leaf that owns the key. Here the tree allows at most three keys per node, so a fourth forces a split.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The overflowed leaf splits into two halves, and the smallest key of the new right leaf is copied up into the parent.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The parent can now overflow too, and repairs the same way one level up. Nine keys, three splits in this run.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
A split at the root creates a new root. That is the only way a B+ tree grows taller, so the height rises by one and never by more.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Deletion removes the key from its leaf. If the leaf still holds at least the minimum number of keys, nothing else changes.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Underflow means fewer keys than the minimum. The first repair is to borrow from an adjacent sibling that has a key to spare.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
If both siblings are already at the minimum, borrow is impossible. Merge the two nodes into one and drop the separator that divided them.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
A merge can make the parent underfull, so the repair cascades upward. If the root ends with no keys, its only child becomes the root.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A point query fetches one node per level, so its cost is exactly the height. The comparison is in block reads, never in key comparisons.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A range query costs the descent plus one extra read for each additional leaf it must visit. The chain turns a range into a walk.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
GATE 2015 Set 2: for keys from 7 up to but not including 15, the model fetches five nodes — root, one internal node, and three leaves.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Fifteen is excluded, so the last leaf is still needed for thirteen. The bound is on nodes fetched, which is why the answer is five and not four.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Same nine keys, same minimum degree: the B-tree needs three levels and the B+ tree needs two. The model builds both and measures.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The B-tree spends block space on record pointers in every node. The B+ tree spends it on keys, so more keys fit, so the fan-out is larger.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The B+ tree stores nine keys as twelve copies: nine in the leaves and three separators. The B-tree stores nine, once each. That is the price.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
A full scan of a B+ tree is a walk along the leaf chain. In a B-tree it is a traversal. Range queries decided the choice.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: decide which node the question is about, then write that node's byte equation. Internal and leaf equations have different shapes.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: solve the inequality and round down. The largest integer that fits is the order, and the key count is one less.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three, for occupancy: order is maximum keys plus one, and the minimum for a non-root node is ceil(order/2) minus one. The root is exempt.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Steps four and five: for cost, fan-out, then levels, then the record block. For a drawn tree, count the descent plus the leaves scanned.

